data.content:
Here are the idiomatic MSTest replacements:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.Any());                          // or CollectionAssert.AreNotEqual(0, users.Count);

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));
    CollectionAssert.AllAreSatisfied(users, u => u.IsActive); // MSTest v3.x+

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3);
    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);

    // Assert.IsTrue(admin != null);
    Assert.IsNotNull(users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin"));
}
```

**Key improvements:**

| Instead of | Use | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count > 0)` | `Assert.IsTrue(x.Any())` | Clearer intent |
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.All(...))` | `CollectionAssert.AllAreSatisfied()` | Per-item failure messages |
| `Assert.IsTrue(a == b)` | `Assert.AreEqual(expected, actual)` | Shows expected vs actual on failure |
| `Assert.IsTrue(x != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(x)` | Specific failure message |

The biggest win is **better failure messages** — `Assert.AreEqual(3, count)` reports `"Expected: 3, Actual: 5"` while `Assert.IsTrue` just says `"Expected: True, Actual: False"`.

> **Note:** `CollectionAssert.AllAreSatisfied` requires **MSTest v3.8+**. On older versions, stick with `Assert.IsTrue(users.All(...))`.
